---
interface Props {
  orientation?: 'horizontal' | 'vertical';
}
const { orientation = 'horizontal' } = Astro.props;
---
<div class={`seam seam--${orientation}`} role="presentation" aria-hidden="true"></div>

<style>
  /*
    The seam is the Barrier — the site's signature device — so it is drawn
    jagged, not as a ruled line. Gold is Earth 1, violet is Earth 2.

    The jag is an SVG path, but it arrives as a mask rather than as inline
    markup: the colour then stays a CSS linear-gradient over the palette
    tokens (Global Constraints: palette via custom properties, never
    literals), and there is no <linearGradient id> to collide with when a page
    renders more than one seam. `mask-repeat` tiles the path at a fixed size,
    so the teeth keep their shape at any width instead of stretching. The path
    starts and ends at the same mid-line coordinate so the tiles join cleanly.

    Static by design: the plan forbids looping motion, so there is no
    animation here and no @media (prefers-reduced-motion) block to need.

    Where mask-image is unsupported the gradient still paints as a plain bar —
    the previous appearance, and a safe degradation for a decorative element.
  */
  .seam--horizontal {
    height: 16px;
    width: 100%;
    background: linear-gradient(90deg, var(--zalian), var(--balian));
    -webkit-mask-image: var(--seam-jag-h);
    mask-image: var(--seam-jag-h);
    -webkit-mask-size: 240px 16px;
    mask-size: 240px 16px;
    -webkit-mask-repeat: repeat-x;
    mask-repeat: repeat-x;
    opacity: 0.85;
  }
  .seam--vertical {
    width: 16px;
    align-self: stretch;
    background: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zalian), var(--balian));
    -webkit-mask-image: var(--seam-jag-v);
    mask-image: var(--seam-jag-v);
    -webkit-mask-size: 16px 240px;
    mask-size: 16px 240px;
    -webkit-mask-repeat: repeat-y;
    mask-repeat: repeat-y;
    opacity: 0.85;
  }
</style>
